course: language
lesson: 1
title: What language is: sounds, words and rules everyone shares
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Language is a code that a whole group of people shares: sounds (or hand signs, or letters) that stand for meanings, plus rules for putting them together.
explain: Picture a toddler pointing at the fridge and shouting "Juice!"; one word, a pointing finger and a hopeful face are enough, because everyone can see what is going on around the word.
explain: Most words have no natural link to what they mean: English says "cat", Ukrainian says "кіт", and neither is the cat's true name; each group of speakers simply agreed long ago.
explain: Every language has grammar, the rules for fitting words together; "The dog ate the bone" makes sense, "Dog the bone the ate" makes people laugh, and the speakers follow these rules without even thinking.
explain: Everybody has several ways of talking (chatty with mates, careful with the head teacher), and every place has its own local words, from "dreich" in Glasgow to "y'all" in Texas; all of it is real language.
fact: A language is a system of words and rules that people share to pass on meaning.
fact: Context is what is going on around the words; it helps you understand short messages like "Juice!".
fact: Most words are arbitrary: the link between a word and its meaning is a habit of the speakers, not something in nature.
fact: Grammar is the set of rules for how words fit together; everyone who speaks a language already uses its grammar.
fact: In English, word order shows who does what: in "The dog bit the man" the dog does the biting, and in "The man bit the dog" the man does.
fact: The subject is who or what does the action; the object is who or what the action is done to; Ukrainian shows this mostly with word endings instead of word order.
fact: Slang is a real part of language with shared meanings: "bruv" is London slang for mate or brother, "fr" means for real, and "no cap" means no lie.
fact: Experts estimate there are about 7,000 languages in the world; the number is not exact, because it is hard to say where one language ends and another begins.
fact: Many people in the world grow up bilingual (speaking two languages) or multilingual (speaking several).
fact: Babies babble ("ba-ba-ba") long before their first words; it is practice with the sounds of language, and every human group ever found has language.
fact: A register is the style of talk you choose for a situation, like chatty with friends or careful with a teacher; switching registers is a skill, not being fake.
fact: Speech came long before writing, and in English letters and sounds often do not match: "knee" has four letters but only two sounds, n and ee.
fact: Local words belong to real dialects: in Scotland "dreich" means dull, grey and drizzly and "the day" means today; in the southern US "y'all" means you all and "fixin' to" means about to.
exercise: Leo's little sister shouts "Juice!" at the fridge and gets juice. Why did one word work? | The situation filled in the rest of the meaning | One-word messages always work anywhere | Mum guessed it by pure luck | "Juice" is a full sentence on its own in grammar
exercise: What is language? | A shared code of words and rules for passing on meaning | A long list of every word that is printed in a dictionary | Only the way grown-ups write things down | Any noise a person or animal can make
exercise: Why is a cat called "cat" in English and "кіт" in Ukrainian? | Each group of speakers agreed on its own word long ago | Ukrainian copied the English word and changed it | "Cat" is the cat's true name and "кіт" is a nickname | Cats make a sound like "cat" in English countries
exercise: Josh's mate texts "the dog ate my homework fr fr" and Josh replies "no cap?" What does "no cap" mean? | No lie, honestly | No hat allowed | No more messages | No homework today
exercise: Is slang a real part of language? | Yes, it has shared meanings and rules within a group | No, it is just lazy noise with no real meaning or rules at all | Only when a teacher says it in class | Only if it is written in a dictionary first
exercise: Mr Okafor writes "Dog the bone the ate". Why does the class laugh? | The words break English word-order rules | The words are all spelled wrongly on the board | Dogs never eat bones in real life | The sentence has no nouns in it at all
exercise: Who does the biting in "The man bit the dog"? | The man, because he comes before the verb | The dog, because dogs are the ones that bite | Nobody, because the sentence is nonsense | Both of them bite each other at once
exercise: Kai's grandad in Glasgow says "Pure dreich the day, eh?" What does he mean? | The weather is grey and drizzly today | He is very hungry today | It is much too hot and sunny to go outside today | It is too hot to go out today
exercise: About how many languages are spoken in the world? | About 7,000 | About 70 | About 700,000 | Exactly 195, one per country
exercise: Why can't experts give an exact number of languages? | It is hard to say where one language ends and another begins | Nobody has ever travelled around the world to count them all | New languages appear every single day | Most languages are kept secret
exercise: What is a person who speaks two languages called? | Bilingual | Biannual | Bicycle | Bilateral
exercise: Sam's baby cousin says "ba-ba-ba" for ten minutes. What is she doing? | Practising the sounds of language | Making random noise with no purpose | Trying to say "banana" and failing | Copying a sheep she heard
exercise: Ollie says "I'm well hungry, innit" at home and "I am quite hungry" to the head teacher. What is this? | Switching registers, a real language skill | Forgetting how to talk properly in front of grown-ups | Forgetting how to talk properly | Speaking two different languages
exercise: How many sounds are in the word "knee"? | Two | Four | Three | One
exercise: What came first in human history? | Speech | Writing | Both appeared on the same day | Printed books
exercise: Noah's uncle from Texas asks "Y'all fixin' to eat?" What does he mean? | Are you all about to eat? | Are you all fixing the food? | Did you all already eat? | Are you all too full to eat?
exercise: In a made-up language, "tok mila" means "the dog sees", "tok pura" means "the dog runs" and "sen pura" means "the cat runs". What does "sen mila" mean? | the cat sees | the dog sees the cat | the cat runs away | the dog runs to the cat
exercise: "Sheep" has five letters. How many sounds does it have? | Three: sh, ee, p | Five, one per letter | Four: s, h, ee, p | Two: shee and p
test: Your mate goes "slang ain't proper language bruv, it's just noise." What's the best answer? | Slang has shared meanings, so it is real language | He's right, slang has no meaning at all and no rules either | He's right, but only for texting | Slang becomes language only when it is printed
test: Your cousin reckons "cat" is the real name and other languages just copy English. Is she right? | No, words are agreed by each language and none is the true name | Yes, because cats understand English best of all the languages | Yes, because cats understand English best | No, the true name is the Latin one
test: What would happen if English had no word-order rules at all? | You often couldn't tell who did what to whom | Nothing, meaning never depends on order | Only poems and songs would stop making any sense at all | Only poems would stop making sense
test: Grace's headline says "Man bites dog!" Her brother says "same as dog bites man, innit." Is he right? ("Innit" here just asks "isn't it?") | No, swapping who comes before the verb swaps the meaning | Yes, because the very same words are used in both of them | Yes, because dogs bite more often | No, because "bites" changes its spelling
test: Gran goes "back in my day babies just made noise till you taught 'em words." What is true? | Babies practise speech sounds by babbling before words | Gran is right, babbling is meaningless noise with no purpose | Babies only learn words from flashcards | Babies cannot hear speech sounds until age two
test: A Gen Z lad says "lowkey most people only speak one language, it's giving one-language world." ("Lowkey" means "a bit, quietly", "it's giving" means "it seems like".) Is he right? | No, many people in the world grow up speaking two or more | Yes, nearly everyone speaks exactly one | Yes, and speaking two is very rare | No, everyone on Earth speaks exactly three
test: In a cartoon, an alien says "Take me to your leader" and every human understands instantly, in every country. What's wrong? | People in different places speak different languages | Nothing, because everyone on Earth speaks English already | Aliens would speak too quietly to hear | Humans only understand written messages
test: Ollie's mate says "you got two voices, you're well fake." What's the wise reply? | Changing style for the person and place is a skill, not fakeness | He's right, a real person talks exactly the same way to everyone | Only actors are allowed two voices | It's fake only if you do it at school
test: Kai has no idea what "dreich" means. What should he do? | Look at the situation, guess, and then ask | Decide his grandad is speaking badly | Ignore it, because local words aren't real | Reply in a different language
test: Your little brother insists "knee" has four sounds because it has four letters. What do you tell him? | Letters and sounds often don't match in English; it has two sounds | He's right, every letter makes one sound | It has four sounds, but only in Scotland | It has no sounds, because the k is silent
test: Uncle Dave says y'all is just a mistake people make when they don't know better. What's true? | It is a normal part of Southern American English, meaning you all | He is right, it's a spelling mistake | It means "yellow" in Texas | It is only used by cowboys in films
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phenomenon designated "language"? | A system of words and rules held in common by a community for the conveyance of meaning | An inventory of words contained exclusively within printed dictionaries of the nation | The written conventions observed solely by adult persons | Any vocal emission produced by a human or other animal
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the English word "cat" and the Ukrainian word "кіт" differ, notwithstanding their common referent. | The association of word and meaning is established by the convention of each community of speakers | The Ukrainian form constitutes a corruption of the English original | The English form reproduces the natural vocalisation of the animal | One of the two forms is officially deemed incorrect
exam: Given the sentences "The dog bit the man" and "The man bit the dog", select the response consistent with the established facts concerning English grammar. | The position before the verb ordinarily designates the performer of the action | The two sentences are identical in meaning in every respect, whatever the order | Word order in English bears no relation to meaning | The second sentence is ungrammatical and therefore meaningless
exam: In respect of the alteration of a speaker's manner of speech according to the person addressed, which statement is most accurate? | It constitutes a recognised competence, termed the selection of register | It constitutes evidence of insincerity on the part of the speaker | It is restricted to persons who speak more than one language | It is a defect which formal instruction ought to eliminate
